questions.rawText:
Suppose you have L₁ = {I₁, I₂, I₃, I₄, I₅, I₆} and the following transactions:
T₁: {I₁, I₂, I₃, I₆}
T₂: {I₂, I₄, I₅}
T₃: {I₁, I₃, I₄, I₆}
T₄: {I₂, I₃, I₅, I₆}
T₅: {I₁, I₂, I₄, I₅}.
(a) Construct the hash table for all 2-itemsets.
(b) Determine bucket counts.
(c) If min support = 4, specify which buckets are pruned and list the remaining candidate itemsets C₂.

A supermarket dataset has the following transactions:
T1 = {Milk, Bread, Butter}
T2 = {Milk, Bread}
T3 = {Milk, Butter}
T4 = {Bread, Butter}
T5 = {Milk, Bread, Butter}
i) Compute the support for each of the following:
(a) {Milk, Bread}
(b) {Milk, Butter}
(c) {Bread, Butter}
ii) Identify which itemsets are frequent if the minimum support threshold = 60%.
iii) Determine which among them are maximal frequent itemsets.

Consider frequent 1-itemsets:
L₁ = {A, B, C, D, E, F}.
Use the hash function
and the following transactions:
T₁: {A, B, C}
T₂: {A, D, E}
T₃: {B, C, E, F}
T₄: {A, B, E, F}.
i. Construct the hash table H₂ showing bucket addresses, counts, and contents.
ii. If the minimum support is 4, which buckets can be pruned?
